<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sexagesimal">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sexagesimal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd millennium BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Base 60</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sexagesimal, or base 60, is a numeral system with sixty as its base, which originated with the Sumerians in the 3rd millennium BCE and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> it was written with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> signs, using ten as a sub-base, and at first had no symbol for zero, so a number's value sometimes had to be judged from its context. Its twelve divisors simplify many fractions, and it survives in the 60 minutes of an hour and the 360 degrees of a circle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sexagesimal.jpg" alt="Sexagesimal" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bill Casselman · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ybc7289-bw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>3rd millennium BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Sumer</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Western New Guinea (the Ekari)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Otto Neugebauer held that the origins of sexagesimal are not as simple, consistent, or singular in time as they are often portrayed, its use always mixing in a strong undercurrent of decimal notation.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform<span>c. 3300 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">Abacus<span>2700–2300 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy<span>c. 1800 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=sexagesimal">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
